What is Log Rotation?

Log rotation is the process of managing old log files so that logs do not consume excessive disk space.

It can:

Rotate logs
Compress old logs
Archive logs
Remove old logs according to policy
Why is Log Rotation Important?

Without log rotation, log files could continue growing until they consume a large amount of disk space.

Log rotation helps:

Prevent disks from filling
Keep recent logs available
Archive older logs
Compress old logs
Maintain system reliability
logrotate

logrotate is a Linux utility used to automatically manage log files.

Common configuration locations:

/etc/logrotate.conf

and:

/etc/logrotate.d/
Example Log Rotation

Instead of having one huge file:

application.log

the system may maintain:

application.log
application.log.1
application.log.2
application.log.3.gz

Older logs can be compressed and eventually removed according to the configured retention policy.
